            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Worship is the act of ascribing supreme worth to God through reverent adoration, homage, self-surrender, and praise, encompassing both liturgical acts and whole-life obedience.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Worship is defined strictly as bowing down (physical homage) and serving (life allegiance), explicitly distinguished from singing or musical praise.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Reverent adoration, homage, submission, and praise directed to God.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Physical homage (bowing down) and life allegiance (serving), kept on God's terms.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book explicitly excludes singing and the 'song service' from the definition of worship, arguing that Scripture treats worshipping (bowing) and praising (singing) as distinct actions, whereas the broader consensus often groups them together.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None regarding the core concepts of homage and allegiance, but the book rejects the common practice of categorizing musical praise as 'worship' itself.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book agrees with the consensus that worship involves homage and allegiance (reflecting the biblical words for bowing down and serving) but refines the boundary by stripping away the widespread inclusion of singing and musical praise, using scriptural scenes where the two acts are distinguished.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The broader biblical usage of worship may sometimes function as an umbrella term for all gathered acts of devotion, meaning that even if bowing and singing are distinct actions in specific verses, singing can still be offered as a form of worshipful homage.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
